The \textit{hat function} $p_a:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}$ centered at $a=(a_1,...,a_d)$ is given by the expression,
    \begin{equation*}
        p_a(x_1,...,x_d) = \max(0,1-\sum\limits_{l=1}^{d}|x_l-a_l|).
    \end{equation*}

Every set $U_i$ corresponds to an $L_1$ ball and $P(U_i) = \{P(x):p_i(x)>0\}$. Then looking at an arbitrary intersection $U_{ij} = U_i\cap U_j = \{x: p_i(x)>0 \land p_j(x)>0\}$, we can also write $P(U_i\cap U_j) = \{P(x):p_i(x)>0 \land p_j(x)>0\}$. What about $P(U_i)\cap P(U_j)$? We can write out the sets,
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{split}
        &P(U_i)\cap P(U_j) =\\
        &\{P(x):p_i(x)>0\}\cap\{P(x):p_j(x)>0\} = \\
        &\{P(x):p_i(x)>0 \land p_j(x)>0\} =\\
        &P(U_i\cap U_j).
    \end{split}
    \end{equation*}
    Since we can do this for arbitrary intersections this concludes the proof.

The wedge sum of two spaces is defined by $X\vee Y = X\sqcup Y/\sim$, where $\sim$ implies identifying $x_0\sim y_0$ to each other. The image of each $\hat{P}_k$ lives in a separate subspace $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}\subset \mathbb{R}^{m(d+1)}$ and we can consider the disjoint union $\bigsqcup\limits_{k=1}^{m} \hat{P}_k(\mathbb{R}^d)$. 

    However, every $\hat{P}_k$ projects the complement of its support to $\{0\}$, which implies that the images of all $\hat{P}_k$ intersect in the origin. Therefore, we need to identify all $\{\{0\}_1,...,\{0\}_m\}$ (which are separate elements in the disjoint union) with each other. This is equivalent to gluing all $\hat{P}_k(\mathbb{R}^d)$ at the origin, which gives $\bigvee\limits_{k=1}^{m}\hat{P}_k(\mathbb{R}^d) \cong \bigvee\limits^{m} S^d$. Therefore $\tilde{H}_n(\bm{P}) \cong \tilde{H}_n(\bigvee\limits^{m} S^d)$ follows from the homotopy invariance of homology. From the fact that the wedge sum of spaces reduces to the direct sum of their homology groups, we have $\tilde{H}_n(\bigvee\limits_{k=1}^{m}\hat{P}_k(\mathbb{R}^d)) = \bigoplus\limits_{k=1}^{m}\tilde{H}_n(\hat{P}_k(\mathbb{R}^d)) \cong \bigoplus\limits^{m}\tilde{H}_n(S^d)$ and 
    \begin{align*}
        \tilde{H}_n(\bm{P}) = 
        \begin{cases}
            \mathbb{Z}^m & n=d\\
            0 & n\neq d.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

Notice that we can also get bounds for products of wedge sums. Poincaré polynomials have the property that $\mathcal{P}(X\vee Y) = \mathcal{P}(X)+\mathcal{P}(Y)-1$. Combined with the product rule from the Künneth formula $\mathcal{P}(X\times Y) = \mathcal{P}(X)\mathcal{P}(Y)$, the Betti numbers of products of wedge sums are given by, 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{P}(\mathop{\bigtimes}\limits_{r}\bigvee\limits_{n=1}^{N_r} S^{d^r_n}) &= \prod\limits_{r}( \sum\limits_{n=1}\limits^{N_r}\mathcal{P}(S^{d^r_n})-(N_r-1)) \\
        &= \prod\limits_{r}( \sum\limits_{n=1}\limits^{N_r}(1+x^{d_n^r})-(N_r-1)).
    \end{align*}

    With $\beta^r_k = \#\{n: d^r_n=k\}$ being the number of copies of $k$-dimensional spheres in the $r$-th factor, the corresponding architecture realizing such a space is $(\sum_r \max_n(d^r_n), \sum_r \sum_k \beta^{r}_k W_{k}, \sum_r \sum_k \beta^{r}_k (k+1))$

\label{facts}
    Define $g(t) = |t-\tfrac{1}{2}|-|t|$, $h(t) = |t+\frac{1}{2d}|-|t|$ and $\|x\|_1=S$, then the following facts hold.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $q_k(x) = 1-S-g(x_k)$.
        \item $q_{d+1}(x) = 1-S-\sum_l h(x_l)$.
        \item If $S\geq \tfrac{1}{2}$, then $x_k>0$ for every $k\in A(x)$.
        \item If some $p_k(x) =0$, then $S\geq \frac{1}{2}$ and $x_k>0$ for every $k\in A(x)$.
    \end{enumerate}

Let $y=(y_T,y_{d+1})\in \bar{R}_J$ with $y_{d+1}>0$. Our goal will be to show that for every $y_{d+1}$ the set $\mathcal{B} = \{c\in[0,y_{d+1}]: (y_T,c) \in \hat{P}(X)\}$ is a lower set of $[0,y_{d+1}]$ which implies that $\mathcal{B} = [0,y_{d+1}]$ and the straight-line homotopy $H(y,t)=(y_T,(1-t)y_{d+1})$ maps into $\bar{R}_J$. Such a homotopy has the properties of a strong deformation retract. 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $H(y,t)$ is continuous, as can be seen from its formula.
        \item $H(y,t)\in \bar{R}_J$, since the zero pattern outside $J$ never changes and $(1-t)y_{d+1}\in[0,y_{d+1}]=\mathcal{B}$.
        \item It is a strong deformation retraction because $H(y,0) = y$, $H(y,1) = (y_T,0)\in \bar{R}_{J\setminus \{d+1\}}$ and $H((y_T,0),t)=(y_T,0)$ for all $t$ (this is the "strong" condition).
    \end{enumerate}

    We again have to split the proof into two cases depending on how many coordinates of $y_T$ are positive.

    \textbf{Case 1: $2\leq \alpha\leq d-1$.} Let $0<c\in \mathcal{B}$ and $x$ be some preimage of $(y_T,c)$ so that $p_{d+1}(x)=c >0$. By facts 3 and 4 from Lemma \ref{facts}, the active coordinates $k\in A(x)$ are positive or $x_k\in E$. Furthermore, if $x_l\geq\frac{1}{2}$ for some $l\in A(x)$ and $l\neq k \in A(x)$ (such a $k$ exists when $\alpha\geq2$), then we can write the equations $q_k(x) = 1 - \|x-a_k\|_1$ and  $q_{d+1}(x) = 1 - \|x-a_{d+1}\|_1$. Adding the two gives,
    \begin{equation*}
        q_{d+1}(x)+q_k(x) = 2-(\|x-a_k\|_1+\|x-a_{d+1}\|_1).
    \end{equation*}
    The sum of norms can be split by the index-ordered pairs. For the $l$-th entry we get $|x_l|+|x_l+\frac{1}{2d}|=2x_l+\frac{1}{2d}$. For the others we can use the triangle inequality to write $|x_j-a_{k,j}|+|x_j-a_{d+1,j}| \geq |a_{k,j} - a_{d+1,j}|$. Summing all of these terms together gives 
    \begin{equation*}
        q_{d+1}(x)+q_k(x) \leq 2 - (1-\frac{1}{2d}+2x_l+\frac{1}{2d}) = 1-2x_l\leq 0. 
    \end{equation*}
    Therefore the assumption that $x_l\geq\frac{1}{2}$ implies that at least one of the two positive coordinates are negative. This is a contradiction, so $x_l < \frac{1}{2}$ and $\tau(x)_l=E^-$ have to hold. This allows us to write an affine expression for all active coordinates $k\in A(x)$,
    \begin{equation*}
        q_k(x) = \frac{1}{2}-S + 2x_k.
    \end{equation*}
    Notice that the flow we wish to define keeps every coordinate fixed except $y_{d+1}$. Therefore, the active coordinates should not change, the inactive ones can change as long as they don't become active and the $d+1$ coordinate has to move so that $y_{d+1}$ decreases. We take a single inactive coordinate with $\tau(x)_{j_0}\in W$, which exists because otherwise we get $x>0$ and $p_{d+1}(x) = 1- \sum(x_l+\frac{1}{2d}) = 1- S-\frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{2}-S$. From fact 4, this contradicts $p_{d+1}(x) > 0$. A set of flow equations satisfying the conditions above is given by,

    \begin{align}
        &\dot{x}_k = \frac{1}{2}, \quad \dot{p}_k = 1-\dot{S} \text{ for } k\in A(x),\\
        &\dot{x}_{j_0} =  \frac{\alpha-2}{2}, \\
        &\dot{x}_j = 0 \text{ for } j\in T\setminus A(x), j\neq j_0.
    \end{align}
    Observe what this does to the derivative $\dot{S} = \sum_{k\in T}\text{sign}(x_l)\dot{x}_k=\frac{\alpha}{2}-\frac{\alpha-2}{2} = 1$, where we used $x_k>0$ for $k\in A(x)$, $x_{j_0}<0$ and $\dot{x}_l=0$ for $l\neq j_0$ and $l\notin A(x)$. This implies that $\dot{p}_k = 0$ for $k\in A(x)$. Additionally for $l\notin A(x)$, we have $\dot{q}_{l} = -\dot{S}=-1$ since $g(x_{j_0}) = \frac{1}{2}$, which means that $p_{l}$ including $p_{j_0}$ stays $0$ for small $t$. What remains to be shown is that $p_{d+1}(x)$ decreases along the flow. There are two cases to separate depending on whether $\tau(x)_{j_0} =W^-$ or $W^+$. 

    By applying fact 2 we have $\dot{p}_{d+1} = -\dot{S}-\sum_{l\in T}\dot{h}(x_l)$. One can see that the only contributing term is $j_0$ from the derivatives of $h(x_l)$,

    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\partial h(x_l)}{\partial x_l} = 
        \begin{cases}
            0 \text{ for } \tau(x)_l = W^-,
            \\
            2 \text{ for } \tau(x)_l = W^+,
            \\
            0 \text{ for } \tau(x)_l \in E.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    Then if $\tau(x)_{j_0} =W^-$, we have $\dot{h}(x_{j_0})=\frac{\partial h(x_{j_0})}{\partial x_{j_0}}\frac{\partial x_{j_0}}{\partial t} = 0$ and $\dot{p}_{d+1} = -\dot{S}=-1$. If $\tau(x)_{j_0} = W^+$, then we have $\dot{h}(x_{j_0})=2\dot{x}_{j_0}$ and $\dot{p}_{d+1} = -\dot{S}-2\dot{x}_{j_0} = 1-\alpha$. In both cases this derivative is negative so $y_{d+1}=p_{d+1}$ decreases along the flow. Using this property we can now prove that $\mathcal{B} = [0,y_{d+1}]$.

    First off, $\hat{P}(x)$ vanishes outside of $\Omega$ so it is compact, which implies that $\mathcal{B}$ is a closed subset. Let $m^* = \inf\{c: [c,y_{d+1}]\subseteq \mathcal{B}\}$, by the closed property we have $[m^*,y_{d+1}]\subseteq \mathcal{B}$. Assume that $m^*>0$ and apply the flow at a preimage of $(y_T,m^*)$ which moves the point to $(y_T,m^*-\delta)$ and therefore $[m^*-\delta,y_{d+1}]\subseteq \mathcal{B}$.  This is a contradiction, so $m^*=0$ and $\mathcal{B}=[0,y_{d+1}]$ and $H(y,t) = (y_T,(1-t)y_{d+1})$ maps into $\bar{R}_J$ and is a strong deformation retract onto the simplex $\bar{R}_{J\setminus\{d+1\}}$.



    \textbf{Case 2:} $\alpha\leq 1$. Using the previous flow equations does not work for $\alpha=1$ as then we cannot derive that active coordinates have $x_k\leq\frac{1}{2}$ and get $\dot{p}_{d+1} = 0$ in the $W^+$ regime. This setting only has two equations to keep track of so we opt for a more explicit approach where we show that the set $\bar{R}_{1,d+1}\setminus \bar{R}_{d+1} = \{y_1e_1+y_{d+1}e_{d+1}:y_1>0 \text{ and } 0\leq y_{d+1}\leq \min(1-y_1,1-\tfrac{1}{d})\}$ is a lower set with respect to $y_{d+1}$. For the bounds, take $p_1(x)>0$, by the triangle inequality we have the sum, 
    \begin{align*}
        q_1(x)+q_{d+1}(x) &= 2 - (\|x-a_1\|_1+\|x-a_{d+1}\|_1) \\
        &\leq 2-\|a_1-a_{d+1}\|_1=1,
    \end{align*}

    hence $p_{d+1}(x) \leq1-p_1(x)$. Then using facts 2 and 4 we can also write $q_{d+1}(x) = 1-S - \sum h(x_k) = 1-S-\tfrac{1}{2d}-\sum_{l\neq 1}h(x_l)$. From the expression of $h$ we have $h(x_l)\geq -\tfrac{1}{2d}$ and $S\geq \frac{1}{2}$ we have, 
    \begin{align*}
        q_{d+1}(x) &\leq 1-S-\tfrac{1}{2d}+\tfrac{d-1}{2d}
        \\
        &\leq \tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{d-2}{2d}=1-\tfrac{1}{d},
    \end{align*}
    therefore $p_{d+1}(x) = \max(0,q_{d+1})\leq 1- \tfrac{1}{d}$
    Then to realize the points satisfying both bounds we take the same approach as in Lemma \ref{Simplex lemma}. Specifically, we solve $(p_1,p_{d+1})$ for a point $x=(x_1,s,...,s)$. When $\tau(x)_1 = E^-$ and $s\in W^-$, we get the solutions,
    \begin{align*}
        x_1 = \frac{1}{2}(y_1-y_{d+1}+1-\tfrac{1}{d}), && s = -\frac{2-\tfrac{1}{d}-y_1-y_{d+1}}{2(d-1)}
    \end{align*}
    One can check these solutions apply when $y_{d+1}\geq y_1-\tfrac{1}{d}$. Otherwise, we consider $\tau(x)_1 = E^+$ and $s\in W^+$ which gives the solutions,
    \begin{align*}
        x_1 = 1-\frac{1}{2}(y_1+y_{d+1}), && s = -\frac{1-y_1+y_{d+1}}{2(d-1)}.
    \end{align*}
    As one can check these solutions are consistent with $y_{d+1} \leq y_1-\frac{1}{d}$. This argument applies for any $\bar{R}_{k,d+1}$ due to the symmetry of the construction.

    Finally, for $\alpha=0$ we have $\bar{R}_{d+1} = \{y_{d+1}e_{d+1}: 0\leq y_{d+1} \leq 1\}$. This is a lower set wrt. $y_{d+1}$ realized by $x = -\lambda\sum e_l$ with $\lambda \in [\frac{1}{2d},\frac{1}{2d}+\frac{1}{d}]$. Every $q_k(x) = \frac{1}{2}-d\lambda\leq 0$ and $p_{d+1}(x) = 1- d(\lambda-\frac{1}{2d})$ where at $\lambda=\frac{1}{2d}$ we have $p_{d+1}(x) = 1$ and at $\lambda = \frac{1}{2d}+\frac{1}{d}$, we have $p_{d+1}(x)=0$. Since we know that $p_{d+1}(x)\leq 1$ this completes the proof.

    Every $y\in\bar R_J$ has $0\leq\alpha\leq d-1$, so by the cases above
$\mathcal S=[0,y_{d+1}]$ for every $y\in\bar R_J$, i.e.\ $\bar R_J$ is a lower set with
respect to $y_{d+1}$. Hence items~1--3 hold, and $H$ is a strong deformation retraction of
$\bar R_J$ onto $\bar R_{J\setminus\{d+1\}}$.


    To conclude, for any $y\in \bar{R}_J$, we have $\mathcal{B}=[0,y_{d+1}]$ for all cases $0\leq\alpha\leq d-1$. This implies $\bar{R}_{J}$ is a lower set wrt. $y_{d+1}$ and all three properties of $H(y,t)$ hold. This implies that $H(y,t)$ is a strong deformation retract onto the simplex $\bar{R}_{J\setminus \{d+1\}}$.

It is again easier if we split the proof depending on whether $d+1 \in J$.

    \textbf{Step 1:} $d+1\notin J$.

    On $\bar{V}_I$ we have $S\leq \frac{1}{2}$ and  on $\bar{R}_J$, with $J=T$, we have $S\geq \frac{1}{2}$. Therefore, on their intersection we have $S=\frac{1}{2}$ and $p_{d+1}(x)=0$. In addition, fact 3 implies that $S = \sum x_k=\frac{1}{2}$ and we can write the equations on this intersection as,

    \begin{align*}
        p_k(x) = 2x_k, \quad p_{d+1}(x) = 0.
    \end{align*}

    From here we see that $\sum p_k(x) = 2\sum x_k$, which by the fact that $\sum x_k =\frac{1}{2}$ implies that $\sum_k p_k(x) = 1$. Furthermore, $p_k(a_k) = e_k$ and $p_l(a_k) = 0$ when $l\neq k$, meaning that the image under $\hat{P}$ is the simplex. Choosing $J\subset T$, simply restricts the simplex to some lower-dimensional face. In any case these intersections are convex and contractible simplices.

    \textbf{Step 2:} $d+1\in J$.

    Denote the base of $\bar{R}_J\cap\bar{V}_I$ , excluding $p_{d+1}$, by $\Delta_J = \{y_k\geq 0: \sum\limits_{k\in J\setminus \{d+1\}} y_k\leq 1\}$. $\Gamma \subset \bar{R}_J\cap\bar{V}_I$ is a graph over this base in which, for $y\in \Delta_J$, points in the intersection are given by $(y,y_{d+1})$. Our goal is to show that $\bar{R}_J\cap\bar{V}_I$ deformation retracts to such a graph $\Gamma\simeq \Delta_J$.  We can write the expression for $p_{d+1}(x) = y_{d+1}$ as,

    \begin{align*}
        p_{d+1}(x) &= \frac{1}{2}-\sum h(x_l) \\
        &= \frac{1}{2}-\frac{d}{2d} + 2\sum\limits \min(x_l^-,\tfrac{1}{2d}) \\
        &= 2\sum \min(x_l^-,\tfrac{1}{2d}).
    \end{align*}


    We can decompose every $x\in \Sigma_J = \{x: x_l\leq 0 \text{ for } l\notin J, \|x\|_1 = \frac{1}{2}\}$ into its positive and negative parts. Then for some index $j_0\in T\setminus J$ (which exists since $J\neq I$), we can write a new point as $\hat{x} = x^+-\|x^-\|_1 e_{j_0}$. Since the $l_1$ norm splits componentwise, the norm of this new point is still $\|\hat{x}\|_1 = \|x^+\|_1+\|x^-\|_1 = \frac{1}{2}$ which means that $\hat{x} \in \Sigma_J$. The positive parts of $x$ and $\hat{x}$ are the same so we get $p_k(x) = p_k(\hat{x}) = 2x_k$. For $p_{d+1}$ the positive parts contribute $0$ and we have $p_{d+1}(\hat{x})= 2\min(\|x^-\|_1,\frac{1}{2d})$. The positive sum $\|x^+\|_1$ can also be written as $\frac{1}{2}\sum y_l=\|x^+\|_1$, which gives $\|x^-\|_1 = \frac{1}{2}-\|x^+\|_1=\frac{1}{2}(1-\sum y_l)$. So $p_{d+1}(\hat{x})$ can be realized through the image coordinates $y$ as $p_{d+1}(\hat{x}) = \min(1-\sum y_l,\frac{1}{d})$. This last expression will be useful later so we use a new function $z^*(y) = \min(1-\sum y_l,\frac{1}{d})$ and define $\Gamma$ as,

    \begin{equation*}
        \Gamma = \{(y,z^*(y)):y\in \Delta_J\}.
    \end{equation*}

    Since they share signs, $x$ and $\hat{x}$ are in the same closed orthant on which $\|x\|_1$ is linear. Therefore, the segment between them stays in a convex set . Let this segment be parametrized as $x(s) = (1-s)x+s\hat{x}$. The image of the positive maps on this path is constant by,

    \begin{align*}
        p_k(x(s)) &= 2x_k^+(s) = 2\max((1-s)x_k+s\hat{x}_k,0)\\
        &=2(1-s)x_k+2sx_k = 2(1-s)\hat{x}_k+2s\hat{x}_k \\&= 2\hat{x}{_k} = p_k(\hat{x}(s)).
    \end{align*}
    The remaining negative coordinates map to $0$ and for $p_{d+1}$ we have a continuous function,
    \begin{align*}
        p_{d+1}(x(s)) = 2\sum \min(x_l(s)^-,\tfrac{1}{2d}).
    \end{align*}

    By substitution, we can check that $p_{d+1}(x(0)) = p_{d+1}(x)$ and $p_{d+1}(x(1)) = z^*(y)$. Given the continuity of $p_{d+1}(x(s))$, any value in the interval $[z^*(y),p_{d+1}(x)]$ is realized for some $s$. By the fact that there is at least one negative coordinate we have $0<2\min(x_l^-,\frac{1}{2d})$ for some $l$, which implies that both extremes of this interval are positive. Therefore, $\hat{P}(x(s))$ stays in $\bar{R}_J\cap\bar{V}_I$.

    Finally, we can define a homotopy $G((y,z),s) = (y,(1-s)z+sz^*(y))$ on the image. It is continuous, $G((y,z),0) = (y,z)$ is the identity and since $\Delta_J$ is fixed throughout, $G((y,z),1)= (y,z^*(y))$ maps into a graph $\Gamma$ over $\Delta_J$. When restricted to $z^*(y)\in\Gamma$, the homotopy keeps $\Gamma$ fixed, since $G((y,z^*(y)),s) = (y,(1-s)z^*(y)+sz^*(y))=(y,z^*(y))$. Since the base is a convex set, hence contractible, it is enough to establish that  $\Gamma\simeq \Delta_J$. This can be done through the projection $(y,z)\to y$, which is continuous and has a continuous inverse given by $y\to (y,z^*(y))$. Therefore, the homotopy $G$ is a deformation retract of $\bar{V}_I\cap \bar{R}_J$ to $\Gamma$ which is homeomorphic to a convex set $\Delta_J$ and is therefore contractible. This concludes the proof.
